# Command

A searchable menu of grouped actions and destinations, filtered as
the user types.

<%= embed :command, :default %>

## When to use

- A jump-anywhere menu over many destinations or actions, grouped by
  area.
- Power-user access to actions that are otherwise spread across
  pages.

## When not to use

- Choosing a *value* for a form — `combobox` submits; command
  navigates and acts.
- A handful of actions — plain buttons or links; a search layer over
  five items is ceremony.
- The ⌘K global palette — that combines `command` with the `dialog`
  primitive and is built per app, not shipped here.

## Usage

`render_command` (`Gesso::Components::CommandHelper`) renders the grouped
command menu; behaviour comes from basecoat's vendored `command.js`,
filtering client-side.

```erb
<%%= render_command(
  placeholder: "Search…",
  groups: [
    {
      label: "Navigation",
      items: [
        { label: "Patients", href: "/patients", icon: "users" },
        { label: "Settings", href: "/settings", icon: "settings" }
      ]
    }
  ]
) %>
```

| Param | Default | Notes |
| ----- | ------- | ----- |
| `groups` | `[]` | `{ label:, items: [{ label:, href:, icon: }] }`; `icon` is an `icons/` svg name |
| `placeholder` | `"Search…"` | search input placeholder |
| `empty` | basecoat's "No results found." | no-match text, via `data-empty` |

## Do and don't

- Do group items by where they live in the product, with group
  labels matching sidebar section names.
- Do give items their established lucide icons — recognition beats
  reading in a fast-filter list.
- Don't mix destructive actions into a command list without their
  confirmation dialogs behind them.
- Don't duplicate every page link into command; it earns its place
  by covering the *deep* destinations search would otherwise miss.

## Accessibility and content

- Renders `role="menu"` with `role="group"` / `role="heading"` /
  `role="menuitem"` structure; filtering and keyboard movement come
  from the basecoat pattern.
- Item labels are the destination or action name as it appears at
  the target — no clever synonyms; users type what they remember.
- The empty state (`empty:`) follows filtered-to-nothing copy: "No
  matching actions."
